<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Braccio da Montone</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1368">1368</a>–<a href="/years/1424">1424</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Andrea Fortebraccio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Braccio da Montone was an Italian condottiere from Perugia, one of the most famous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> captains of his age. He seized Perugia in 1416 and ruled it as lord, also extending his power over much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a>. He was mortally wounded in 1424 while besieging L'Aquila against the forces of the pope and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/braccio-da-montone.jpg" alt="Braccio da Montone" width="320" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cristofano dell'Altissimo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Braccio_da_Montone,_portrait_of_Cristofano_dell%27Altissimo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Andrea Fortebraccio on 1 July 1368, the son of the nobleman Oddo Fortebraccio and Giacoma Montemelini, Braccio began as a page in the company of Guido d'Asciano. When his family was exiled from Perugia and lost the castle of Montone, he joined Alberico da Barbiano's Company of St. George, where he became a friend of Muzio Attendolo Sforza. He was slightly crippled at the siege of Fossombrone in 1391 and served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, the Pope and Barbiano in turn, until companions accused him of plotting to kill Alberico and he had to flee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1406 he led a company formed largely of exiles from Perugia, and in May 1407 Rocca Contrada gave him the lordship of the town. He served King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-of-naples">Ladislaus of Naples</a>, then turned against him, taking Jesi, and in 1410 he defeated the retreating Neapolitan troops at Sora. The speed of his troops became proverbial, and a school of the condottiere's art, the Braccesca, was named after him. The antipope John XXIII gave him Montone and the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, which he used to extort large sums from the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a>.</p><p>After Ladislaus died in 1414 Braccio gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> for 180,000 gold ducats and conquered most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>. He defeated Carlo Malatesta's forces at Sant'Egidio on 12 July 1416, a battle later painted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paolo-uccello">Paolo Uccello</a>, and entered Perugia; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todi">Todi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narni">Narni</a>, Orvieto and Terni also took him as lord. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-martin-v">Pope Martin V</a> sent two armies against him, which he defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a>, and on 14 March 1419 the pope named him papal vicar in return for driving Antongaleazzo Bentivoglio out of Bologna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When the pope excommunicated Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joanna-ii-of-naples">Joanna II of Naples</a>, she named Alfonso of Aragon her heir and hired Braccio, who faced Sforza again in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a> and began a thirteen-month siege of L'Aquila. On 3 February 1424 he was made Gran Conestabile of the kingdom. When Joanna went over to the Angevins he stayed loyal to Alfonso. On 2 June he was wounded in the neck below the walls of L'Aquila and taken prisoner, and he died three days later. Having died excommunicated, he was buried in unconsecrated ground until his nephew Niccolò Fortebraccio moved his body to San Francesco al Prato in Perugia in 1432.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Braccio da Montone” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 15th century the condottiere <b>Braccio da Montone</b> ruled the city, and later the Baglioni family dominated it.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-perugia">Commune of Perugia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Perugia, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in L'Aquila, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.11,12.39&amp;year=1404" aria-label="Perugia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Braccio da Montone"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5767 3847h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5767 3847h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.11,12.39&amp;year=1404">What was here in 1404? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
